(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["principles-of-diagnostic-medicine-i-exam-1-lab-interpretation-chemistry-urinalysis-quiz"]={"title":"Lab Interpretation 2: Chemistry & Urinalysis — PDM I Exam 1","category":"Principles of Diagnostic Medicine I","exam":"Exam 1","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A 19-year-old with type 1 diabetes has vomiting and deep rapid breathing. Her results are glucose 512 mg/dL (70–99) · bicarbonate 8 mEq/L (22–29) · sodium 132 mEq/L (135–145) · chloride 96 mEq/L (98–107) · urine ketones large (negative). What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Diabetic ketoacidosis","Hyperosmolar hyperglycemic state","Lactic acidosis","Diarrhoeal bicarbonate loss"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. High glucose, low bicarbonate and ketones, with an anion gap of 132 − (96 + 8) = 28 against a normal of 8 to 12. The unmeasured acids are the ketones, and the deep rapid breathing is respiratory compensation. — 5. Chemistry Panels, Renal Fxn, Elytes.pptx, anion gap and metabolic pattern"},{"q":"An 82-year-old man has had three days of vomiting and poor intake. He is hypotensive. His results are blood urea nitrogen 62 mg/dL (7–20) · creatinine 1.6 mg/dL (0.6–1.2) · sodium 148 mEq/L (135–145) · urine specific gravity 1.032 (1.005–1.030). What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Intrinsic renal failure","Postrenal obstruction","Syndrome of inappropriate antidiuretic hormone","Prerenal azotemia"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The urea to creatinine ratio is about 39 to 1, well above 20 to 1, which places the problem BEFORE the kidney — poor perfusion. The concentrated urine confirms the tubules are working hard to retain water. — 5. Chemistry Panels, Renal Fxn, Elytes.pptx, urea to creatinine ratio"},{"q":"A 58-year-old woman is oliguric after several days of an aminoglycoside. Her results are blood urea nitrogen 48 mg/dL (7–20) · creatinine 4.2 mg/dL (0.6–1.2) · potassium 5.9 mEq/L (3.5–5.0) · bicarbonate 17 mEq/L (22–29). What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Prerenal azotemia","Intrinsic renal failure","Diabetic ketoacidosis","Diabetes insipidus"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The ratio is about 11 to 1 — below 20 to 1 — so the damage is in the kidney itself, and the drug is the obvious insult. The high potassium and low bicarbonate are the expected consequences of failing excretion. — 5. Chemistry Panels, Renal Fxn, Elytes.pptx, urea to creatinine ratio"},{"q":"A 24-year-old woman has had a week of vomiting from hyperemesis. Her results are sodium 131 mEq/L (135–145) · potassium 2.9 mEq/L (3.5–5.0) · chloride 88 mEq/L (98–107) · bicarbonate 36 mEq/L (22–29). What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Diabetic ketoacidosis","Diarrhoeal bicarbonate loss","Metabolic alkalosis from protracted vomiting","Syndrome of inappropriate antidiuretic hormone"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Sodium, potassium and chloride all down with bicarbonate UP is the vomiting panel. It persists because volume, potassium and chloride depletion force the kidney to keep reabsorbing bicarbonate — which is why it does not correct until chloride and potassium are replaced. — 5. Chemistry Panels, Renal Fxn, Elytes.pptx, the vomiting case"},{"q":"A 68-year-old man with small cell lung cancer is confused. He looks euvolaemic. His results are sodium 118 mEq/L (135–145) · serum osmolality 248 mOsm/kg (275–295) · urine osmolality 580 mOsm/kg (—) · urine sodium 62 mEq/L (—). What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Syndrome of inappropriate antidiuretic hormone","Pseudohyponatraemia","Diabetes insipidus","Prerenal azotemia from volume depletion"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A genuinely low serum osmolality with inappropriately CONCENTRATED urine — the kidney should be making dilute urine to shed water and is doing the opposite. Small cell lung cancer is a classic source. — 5. Chemistry Panels, Renal Fxn, Elytes.pptx, fluid balance"},{"q":"A patient with uncontrolled diabetes has these results sodium 128 mEq/L (135–145) · glucose 700 mg/dL (70–99) · serum osmolality 300 mOsm/kg (275–295). How should the sodium be interpreted?","choices":["It is diluted by the glucose and corrects once that is treated","It represents true water excess needing fluid restriction","It is a laboratory artefact from lipaemic serum","It indicates the syndrome of inappropriate antidiuretic hormone"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Glucose pulls water into the vascular space, lowering measured sodium by roughly 1.6 to 2 mEq/L for every 100 mg/dL above normal — about 10 here. The osmolality is HIGH, which tells you the patient is not water-overloaded at all. — 5. Chemistry Panels, Renal Fxn, Elytes.pptx, three pitfalls"},{"q":"A 34-year-old woman has malaise and dark urine for a week. Her results are aspartate aminotransferase 1,840 units/L (10–40) · alanine aminotransferase 2,210 units/L (7–56) · alkaline phosphatase 128 units/L (44–147) · total bilirubin 6.2 mg/dL (0.2–1.2). What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Alcoholic hepatitis","Acute viral hepatitis","Common bile duct obstruction","Chronic hepatitis C"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Transaminases in the THOUSANDS have only three causes — viral, ischaemic and toxic — and the alkaline phosphatase is barely moved, making this hepatocellular rather than cholestatic. Alanine exceeding aspartate fits a viral picture. — 5. Chemistry Panels, Renal Fxn, Elytes.pptx, hepatic patterns"},{"q":"A 61-year-old woman has painless jaundice and pale stools. Her results are alkaline phosphatase 612 units/L (44–147) · gamma-glutamyl transferase 488 units/L (9–48) · alanine aminotransferase 74 units/L (7–56) · total bilirubin 9.4 mg/dL (0.2–1.2). What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Acute viral hepatitis","A cholestatic process obstructing bile flow","Paget disease of bone","Alcoholic hepatitis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Alkaline phosphatase raised far out of proportion to the transaminases is the cholestatic signature, and the high gamma-glutamyl transferase confirms the alkaline phosphatase is hepatic rather than bone. — 5. Chemistry Panels, Renal Fxn, Elytes.pptx, cholestatic pattern"},{"q":"A 49-year-old man who drinks heavily has tender hepatomegaly. His results are aspartate aminotransferase 186 units/L (10–40) · alanine aminotransferase 72 units/L (7–56) · alkaline phosphatase 132 units/L (44–147) · mean corpuscular volume 104 fL (80–100). What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Acute viral hepatitis","Biliary obstruction","Acetaminophen toxicity","Alcoholic liver disease"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Aspartate above alanine by more than two to one is the alcohol ratio, the magnitude is modest rather than in the thousands, and the macrocytosis alongside it is the same story from the blood count. — 5. Chemistry Panels, Renal Fxn, Elytes.pptx, AST to ALT ratio"},{"q":"A 57-year-old man with known cirrhosis is assessed. His results are albumin 2.4 g/dL (3.5–5.0) · international normalised ratio 2.1 (0.9–1.1) · alanine aminotransferase 48 units/L (7–56) · total bilirubin 3.8 mg/dL (0.2–1.2). What do these results show?","choices":["Impaired synthetic function rather than active injury","Severe acute hepatocellular injury","Biliary obstruction","Dehydration concentrating the samples"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Albumin and clotting factors are MADE by the liver, so a low albumin and a prolonged clotting time measure how well it works. The near-normal transaminases show little ongoing hepatocyte injury — function and injury are separate axes. — 5. Chemistry Panels, Renal Fxn, Elytes.pptx, injury against function"},{"q":"A 31-year-old man has had five days of watery diarrhoea. His results are sodium 138 mEq/L (135–145) · chloride 116 mEq/L (98–107) · bicarbonate 14 mEq/L (22–29) · potassium 3.1 mEq/L (3.5–5.0). What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["A high anion gap metabolic acidosis","Metabolic alkalosis","A normal anion gap metabolic acidosis from bicarbonate loss","Diabetic ketoacidosis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The gap is 138 − (116 + 14) = 8, which is normal, so no unmeasured acid has appeared — bicarbonate has simply been lost from the gut and chloride has risen to keep electrical neutrality. Hence hyperchloraemic. — 5. Chemistry Panels, Renal Fxn, Elytes.pptx, normal gap acidosis"},{"q":"A patient's panel returns with these results sodium 140 mEq/L (135–145) · chloride 98 mEq/L (98–107) · bicarbonate 12 mEq/L (22–29). Calculate the anion gap and say what it means.","choices":["30 — a normal gap, meaning bicarbonate loss","30 — a raised gap, meaning unmeasured acids","12 — a normal gap, meaning bicarbonate loss","42 — a raised gap, meaning unmeasured acids"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. 140 − (98 + 12) = 30, against a normal of 8 to 12. A gap this wide means acid has been added rather than bicarbonate lost, and the MUDPILES list is where you go next. — 5. Chemistry Panels, Renal Fxn, Elytes.pptx, anion gap"},{"q":"A 66-year-old woman on a potassium-sparing diuretic and an angiotensin converting enzyme inhibitor has muscle weakness. Her results are potassium 6.8 mEq/L (3.5–5.0) · creatinine 2.1 mg/dL (0.6–1.2) · bicarbonate 19 mEq/L (22–29) · electrocardiogram peaked T waves (—). What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Hypokalaemia from diuretic use","Metabolic alkalosis driving potassium into cells","Hyperkalaemia requiring urgent treatment","A laboratory artefact from haemolysed sample"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Potassium is excreted by the kidney with essentially no reabsorption, so reduced clearance plus two drugs that block its excretion is a common route to this. Small serum changes have large physiological effects, and the peaked T waves prove the membrane is already affected. — 5. Chemistry Panels, Renal Fxn, Elytes.pptx, potassium"},{"q":"A 24-year-old woman has dysuria, frequency and urgency. Her urine dipstick shows leukocyte esterase positive (negative) · nitrites positive (negative) · blood trace (negative) · protein trace (negative or trace). What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Urinary tract infection","Glomerulonephritis","Rhabdomyolysis","Diabetes mellitus"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Leukocyte esterase positive means pyuria — white cells are present — and a positive nitrite means bacteria have been converting urinary nitrates for more than four hours. Together with the symptoms that is an infection. — 6. Urinalysis Diagnostics SV Gopal Fall 2026.pptx, the two infection pads"},{"q":"A 30-year-old woman has clear dysuria and frequency. Her dipstick shows leukocyte esterase positive (negative) · nitrites NEGATIVE (negative) · blood trace (negative). How should the negative nitrite be read?","choices":["It excludes bacterial infection, so no culture is needed","It means the sample was contaminated","It does not exclude infection, and culture should still be sent","It means the infection is fungal rather than bacterial"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Nitrite sensitivity is only about 50%, and the commonest organism is rarely a nitrite producer, so a negative pad is weak evidence. With symptoms and a positive leukocyte esterase, send culture and sensitivity. — 6. Urinalysis Diagnostics SV Gopal Fall 2026.pptx, nitrite sensitivity"},{"q":"A 12-year-old has cola-coloured urine and puffy eyes two weeks after a sore throat. His urinalysis shows blood large (negative) · protein 2+ (negative or trace) · microscopy dysmorphic red cells and red cell casts (—) · blood pressure 148/92 (—). What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Glomerulonephritis","Urinary tract infection","Nephrolithiasis","Rhabdomyolysis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Blood AND protein together, with red cell casts, localises the bleeding to the glomerulus — casts form in the tubule, so the cells came from above it. The preceding pharyngitis and hypertension complete the picture. — 6. Urinalysis Diagnostics SV Gopal Fall 2026.pptx, blood and protein"},{"q":"A 26-year-old man is found after a long period immobile on the floor. His urine is dark. Results are urine blood large (negative) · urine microscopy no red blood cells seen (0–2 per field) · creatine phosphokinase 48,000 units/L (30–200) · potassium 6.1 mEq/L (3.5–5.0). What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Haematuria from a kidney stone","Haemoglobinuria from intravascular haemolysis","Myoglobinuria from rhabdomyolysis","Urinary tract infection"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The pad detects heme, so it cannot tell red cells, free haemoglobin and myoglobin apart. A strongly positive pad with NO red cells means one of the latter two, and the creatine phosphokinase names it as muscle. — 6. Urinalysis Diagnostics SV Gopal Fall 2026.pptx, the three meanings of blood"},{"q":"A 34-year-old woman with sickle cell disease has dark urine and jaundice. Results are urine blood large (negative) · urine microscopy no red blood cells seen (0–2 per field) · unconjugated bilirubin 5.2 mg/dL (0.2–0.8) · creatine phosphokinase 112 units/L (30–200). What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Myoglobinuria from rhabdomyolysis","Haemoglobinuria from intravascular haemolysis","Haematuria from glomerulonephritis","Bilirubinuria from biliary obstruction"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Pad positive with no red cells again — but here the muscle enzyme is normal and the unconjugated bilirubin is high, which is a direct product of haemoglobin breakdown. That is the confirming test for this pigment. — 6. Urinalysis Diagnostics SV Gopal Fall 2026.pptx, haemoglobinuria"},{"q":"A 22-year-old woman has lost weight and feels unwell. Her dipstick shows ketones large (negative) · glucose 3+ (negative) · specific gravity 1.028 (1.005–1.030) · protein negative (negative or trace). What is the immediate next step?","choices":["Send urine culture and sensitivity","Arrange a twenty-four hour urine collection","Check the blood glucose","Repeat the dipstick in two weeks"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Ketones mean cells are burning fatty acids instead of glucose, and the stated next move is always to check the blood sugar. With glucose also spilling into the urine, uncontrolled diabetes is the working diagnosis until that number comes back. — 6. Urinalysis Diagnostics SV Gopal Fall 2026.pptx, ketones and glucose"},{"q":"A 27-year-old pregnant woman has a routine dipstick. Results are glucose trace (negative) · blood glucose 84 mg/dL (70–99) · protein trace (negative or trace) · ketones negative (negative). What best explains the glucose in the urine?","choices":["Pregnancy lowers the tubular reabsorption threshold","The blood glucose must have been high earlier in the day","The sample is contaminated","This is early diabetic ketoacidosis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Glucose is filtered then wholly reabsorbed proximally until the blood level exceeds the tubular threshold, around 180 mg/dL. With a normal blood glucose, glucose in the urine means the threshold itself has fallen — and in pregnancy a trace is expected. — 6. Urinalysis Diagnostics SV Gopal Fall 2026.pptx, tubular threshold"},{"q":"A 71-year-old man has bone pain and anaemia. His dipstick shows protein negative (negative or trace) · total serum protein 11.2 g/dL (6.0–8.3) · albumin 3.1 g/dL (3.5–5.0) · smear rouleaux (—). Why is the dipstick protein negative, and what should be ordered?","choices":["The kidney is undamaged, so no further urine testing is needed","Repeat the dipstick on a first morning sample","Send a twenty-four hour collection for total protein","Strips miss Bence Jones protein — send urine protein electrophoresis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Reagent strips detect albumin and are insensitive to the light chains of a paraprotein, so a negative pad does not exclude them. With rouleaux and a total protein of 11.2, electrophoresis is the test that finds them. — 6. Urinalysis Diagnostics SV Gopal Fall 2026.pptx, the myeloma trap"},{"q":"A patient has a computed tomography study with intravenous contrast, and a urinalysis is sent afterwards. Results are specific gravity 1.048 (1.005–1.030) · protein negative (negative or trace) · glucose negative (negative) · blood negative (negative). What best explains the specific gravity?","choices":["Radiographic contrast, whose large particles inflate the reading","Severe dehydration","Diabetes insipidus","Heavy proteinuria adding solute"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Specific gravity weighs solutes against water, so particle SIZE matters as well as number — marbles against glitter. Contrast has unusually large molecules and characteristically drives the value above 1.040. — 6. Urinalysis Diagnostics SV Gopal Fall 2026.pptx, specific gravity"},{"q":"A 44-year-old man passes large volumes of pale urine and is constantly thirsty. Results are urine specific gravity 1.002 (1.005–1.030) · serum sodium 149 mEq/L (135–145) · glucose 92 mg/dL (70–99) · urine glucose negative (negative). What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Diabetes mellitus with osmotic diuresis","Diabetes insipidus","Syndrome of inappropriate antidiuretic hormone","Psychogenic polydipsia"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Dilute urine while the serum sodium is HIGH is the giveaway — the kidney is shedding water it should be keeping, so antidiuretic effect is missing or unheard. Normal glucose rules out the osmotic alternative. — 6. Urinalysis Diagnostics SV Gopal Fall 2026.pptx, specific gravity low"},{"q":"A 39-year-old woman has recurrent infections with a urea-splitting organism. Results are urine pH 8.2 (4.5–8.0) · nitrites positive (negative) · leukocyte esterase positive (negative) · microscopy coffin-lid crystals (—). What kind of stone is she forming, and what is the treatment principle?","choices":["Uric acid — alkalinise the urine","Struvite — treat the infection","Calcium oxalate — restrict dietary oxalate","Cystine — increase fluid intake alone"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Alkaline urine is produced by urease-producing bacteria splitting urea to ammonia, and struvite and triple phosphate stones form in that environment. The stone is a consequence of the infection, so the infection is the target. — 6. Urinalysis Diagnostics SV Gopal Fall 2026.pptx, pH and stones"},{"q":"A patient with a normal anion gap metabolic acidosis has a urinalysis. Results are serum bicarbonate 16 mEq/L (22–29) · serum chloride 114 mEq/L (98–107) · urine pH 6.8 (4.5–8.0). What does the urine pH suggest?","choices":["Appropriate renal compensation for the acidosis","Diabetic ketoacidosis","A urea-splitting urinary infection","Renal tubular acidosis, because the kidney is failing to excrete acid"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. In an acidosis the urine should be maximally acidic as the kidney dumps hydrogen ions. A pH of 6.8 in that setting means it cannot — which is the name trap: renal tubular ACIDOSIS gives an inappropriately ALKALINE urine. — 6. Urinalysis Diagnostics SV Gopal Fall 2026.pptx, renal tubular acidosis"},{"q":"A 38-year-old man has ankle swelling. His dipstick shows protein 3+ (negative or trace) · blood negative (negative) · serum albumin 2.2 g/dL (3.5–5.0) · leukocyte esterase negative (negative). What is the appropriate next test?","choices":["Urine culture and sensitivity","Repeat the dipstick after exercise","A twenty-four hour urine collection to quantify the protein","Serum creatine phosphokinase"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The strip is semi-quantitative — trace, 1+, 2+ are graded estimates rather than measurements — so heavy proteinuria with a low albumin and oedema needs quantifying before anything else. — 6. Urinalysis Diagnostics SV Gopal Fall 2026.pptx, protein"},{"q":"A urinalysis is reported from a mid-stream sample in a well woman. Results are microscopy more than 20 squamous epithelial cells per high power field (—) · bacteria few (—) · leukocyte esterase negative (negative) · nitrites negative (negative). How should the bacteria be interpreted?","choices":["Significant bacteriuria requiring antibiotics","Probably contamination, and the sample should be repeated","Evidence of pyelonephritis","A urea-splitting infection"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. More than twenty squamous cells per field means the sample picked up skin and genital flora on the way out. With negative leukocyte esterase and nitrites there is no evidence of true infection. — 6. Urinalysis Diagnostics SV Gopal Fall 2026.pptx, after the strip"},{"q":"A well 25-year-old man has a routine urinalysis. Results are specific gravity 1.018 (1.005–1.030) · pH 6.0 (4.5–8.0) · protein, glucose, ketones, blood, bilirubin all negative (negative) · leukocyte esterase and nitrites negative (negative). How should this be reported?","choices":["Abnormal, because the specific gravity should be 1.000","Abnormal, because the pH should be 7.0","Inadequate, because microscopy was not performed","Normal"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Six pads should read negative — leukocyte esterase, nitrites, ketones, glucose, blood and bilirubin — with protein negative or trace. Specific gravity and pH are the two that always carry a value rather than being simply negative, and both sit in range. — 6. Urinalysis Diagnostics SV Gopal Fall 2026.pptx, the reagent strip"},{"q":"A 63-year-old woman with breast cancer has confusion and constipation. Her results are calcium 13.8 mg/dL (8.5–10.5) · albumin 4.1 g/dL (3.5–5.0) · creatinine 1.7 mg/dL (0.6–1.2) · phosphate 2.2 mg/dL (2.5–4.5). What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Pseudohypercalcaemia from a high albumin","Primary hypoparathyroidism","Chronic kidney disease with secondary changes","Hypercalcaemia of malignancy"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A genuinely high calcium with a normal albumin — so it is not a measurement artefact of protein binding — in someone with a known cancer. The low phosphate and rising creatinine fit, and the confusion and constipation are the classic symptoms. — 5. Chemistry Panels, Renal Fxn, Elytes.pptx, minerals"},{"q":"An 84-year-old woman weighing 42 kg has these results creatinine 1.0 mg/dL (0.6–1.2) · blood urea nitrogen 26 mg/dL (7–20) · albumin 2.8 g/dL (3.5–5.0). How should the creatinine be interpreted?","choices":["It confirms normal kidney function","It indicates prerenal azotemia","It is falsely raised by her low albumin","It may conceal reduced filtration, because she has little muscle"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Creatinine comes from muscle metabolism, so an elderly or cachectic patient generates less of it and can hold a normal-looking value while filtration is genuinely impaired. A number inside the range is not the same as a normal kidney. — 5. Chemistry Panels, Renal Fxn, Elytes.pptx, the creatinine trap"}]};
